  "findings": [
    "The scheduler is already isolated in upstream server-context, a position-independent static library. No scheduler extraction or source fork was needed to embed it.",
    "The old installed libllama-server-impl.so also exports server_context and response-reader C++ symbols; the latest proof links only server-context and its library dependencies.",
    "The latest upstream core exposes SERVER_TASK_TYPE_DECISION with explicit token labels and returns raw final-prompt logits without sampling.",
    "Direct decision tasks with label IDs work on the ordinary Gemma-4 E4B GGUF. The public systemone endpoint has additional decision-model metadata requirements and is not claimed to work on this GGUF.",
    "The native C++ API is not a stable C/Rust ABI. Put opaque handles and typed requests/results in a small application-owned C ABI adapter, and pin the upstream revision.",
    "The current batch compatibility check requires matching task types. An active completion batch therefore excludes a decision task, even when a free slot admits it.",
    "A one-token completion request is compatible with running completions and returns pre-sampling probabilities through the same native result queue. This performs token selection but does not feed that token back for another transformer pass.",
    "Application priority lanes and reserved interactive capacity still need an adapter policy; the upstream queue front flag does not supply full application scheduling policy."
  ],
  "recommendation": [
    "Reuse upstream server-context directly, with a small C ABI adapter; keep source upgrades pinned and validated.",
    "For mixed text generation and routing with no fork, use one-token completion-based scoring, retaining candidate probabilities and ignoring the emitted letter if desired.",
    "Use true decision tasks for score-only workloads. Mixing zero-sampling decision tasks into active completion batches requires an upstream compatibility change or a downstream patch; this proof did not modify upstream.",
    "A custom batcher is reasonable for narrowly scoped text-only prompt scoring, but full GLaDOS inference adds streaming, multimodal processing, prefix reuse, SWA checkpoints and cancellation. Reimplementing these is a larger maintenance commitment."
  ],
